<!-- harness:region id=loop -->
## The loop

```
intake → discovery → prioritize → commit → deliver → [ABSORPTION/SECURITY GATE] → learn
   ↑                                                                                 │
   └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

A **loop, not a pipeline**: `learn` feeds the next `intake`. Six phases,
one artifact each.

| Phase | Artifact | What must be true to leave it |
|---|---|---|
| Intake | An entry in `portfolio/register.md`, placed Now / Next / Later | The intake gate field is filled. An idea with no stated intake reason is not an entry |
| Discovery | Evidence notes in the bet folder, plus a draft brief | Every load-bearing claim is classified frame / mechanism / vendor magnitude, with its source |
| Prioritize | A ranked register, cut to **absorbable** capacity | The cut is to what can be absorbed, not to what could be started |
| Commit | A committed brief **with kill criteria**, plus a horizon record | Kill criteria exist **before** delivery starts. The horizon is computed, not chosen |
| Deliver | Milestone progress against the committed brief | Progress is against the brief as committed, not one that quietly moved |
| **Gate** | A gate record: security review, V&V, maintenance capacity, review throughput | A `PASS` or `HOLD` token from a party that did not run deliver |
| Learn | A roadmap review feeding the next intake | The kill-criteria check has run, and the review produced an intake consequence |

**Horizon-setting lives inside commit** and is first-class:

> `horizon ≈ max(longest-lead physical/regulatory constraint,
> capital-commitment irreversibility)`, adjusted **up** when uncertainty
> resolves slowly, **down** when competitive substitution is fast.

Walk it with `/horizon`, which follows
`.harness/pack/templates/horizon-decision-aid.md`. The full phase
definitions are in `.harness/pack/process.md`.
<!-- harness:end -->

<!-- harness:region id=gate -->
## The absorption / security gate — non-delegable

Between `deliver` and `learn`. It asks whether the organisation can
actually take on what was built, across four capacity questions: security
review by someone who did not build it, verification and validation
evidence, maintenance ownership after the bet closes, and review
throughput on top of everything already in flight.

**Three rules, and none of them varies by calibration:**

- It **may not be cleared or waived by the party that ran `deliver`.**
- It **may not run in parallel with `learn`.**
- Its verdict is a **named token — `PASS` or `HOLD` — not prose.**

**An unsupervised run may carry a bet up to the gate. It may never clear
it.** Clearing or waiving the gate is an abort criterion in this
project's target template. A run that reaches the gate stops, writes its
final work-log entry, and hands back.

How much of the gate this organisation already holds structurally is the
one thing the calibration changes about it — read
`portfolio/absorption-gate.md`.
<!-- harness:end -->

<!-- harness:region id=practices -->
## The five practices

Standing instructions. All five hold at both calibrations.

1. **Concentrate the portfolio to absorbable capacity** — not to
   available capacity, and not to what fits on a slide.
2. **Set the horizon by the binding constraint**, not by the planning
   calendar.
3. **Match cadence to uncertainty resolution.** The calendar is a
   backstop, not a trigger.
4. **Keep the security gate non-delegable.**
5. **Budget review capacity as a portfolio input** — review throughput
   constrains how many bets can run.
<!-- harness:end -->

<!-- harness:region id=conventions -->
## Conventions

**Evidence discipline.** Classify every load-bearing claim as **frame**,
**mechanism** or **vendor magnitude**, and label it before you use it. A
frame is never evidence of a magnitude. **Vendor telemetry is never
stacked as independent corroboration** — three vendor numbers pointing
the same way are one interested source three times.

**The claims ledger.** Every load-bearing claim in a brief or a review is
recorded with its source and its class. A claim with no source is written
`[NEEDS SOURCE]` and stays that way. **Never fabricate a citation.**

**Kill criteria before the bet starts.** A brief without kill criteria is
not committed, whatever its status says. They must be observable, dated
or thresholded, and owned. `/bet` refuses to mark a bet committed while
`Kill criteria` is empty or at its placeholder, and `/review` re-checks
every bet it reviews. **That is the whole of the enforcement — it does
not fire on the file write**, and a bet committed by hand-editing
`brief.md` is not blocked. The guard script at
`.claude/hooks/kill-criteria-guard.sh` is **inert**: registered by
nothing, executed by nothing.

**No drift substituting for decision.** A bet that crosses its kill
criteria is killed, or explicitly re-committed with a dated rationale in
`portfolio/decisions.md`. Silence is not a decision and "we kept going"
is not a re-commitment.

**Bet status vocabulary** (provisional):
`proposed | committed | held-at-gate | killed | absorbed`.

Full text: `.harness/pack/conventions.md` and
`.harness/pack/coordination.md`.
<!-- harness:end -->

<!-- harness:region id=roles -->
## Roles — provisional

Six portfolio agents live in `.claude/agents/`: `portfolio-steward`,
`discovery-lead`, `bet-framer`, `horizon-analyst`, `gate-reviewer` and
`learning-synthesiser`. **They are provisional.** The phases, the gate,
the templates and the horizon framework are evidenced; these role
boundaries are inferred from the phases, and the source research flags
engineering-manager portfolio responsibility as genuinely unwritten. Each
file says so at the top. Treat the boundaries as a proposal and revise
them against what this portfolio actually needs.

Two things are **not** provisional: `gate-reviewer` must be a different
party from whoever ran `deliver`, and the gate verdict must be a named
token. Both follow from the process, not from the roles.

`target-reviewer` is a seventh agent and is **not** provisional — it
belongs to the targets contract below.

**Commands:** `/bet` opens a brief, `/review` runs a roadmap review,
`/horizon` walks the decision aid, `/target` runs an unsupervised target.
<!-- harness:end -->

<!-- harness:region id=targets -->
## Autonomous work — targets

Long-running unsupervised work uses a **target**: a measurable goal the
agent works toward on its own, stopping at **SUCCESS** (every criterion
verified) or **ABORT** (a stop condition fired). Never an open-ended
"improve X".

- **Launch:** `/target <target-file>`.
- **Readiness gate:** the `target-reviewer` agent validates a filled
  target and returns `READY` / `NEEDS-CORRECTION` before anything starts.
- **The absorption gate is outside the envelope.** Clearing or waiving it
  is an abort criterion, and `target-reviewer` returns
  `NEEDS-CORRECTION` for any target whose success criteria can only be
  met by passing it.
- **Instances:** filled targets and their work logs are project content
  and live in `targets/`.

The way of working: `.harness/pack/targets/README.md`.
<!-- harness:end -->
